0.6.1a: 
[x] - Fix security vulnerability in webpage cloning agent by removing the agent
[x] - Fix summarization loop having an unbound payload
[x] - Fix network errors
[x] - Fix plain chat mode dying on a mid-stream provider drop (RemoteProtocolError:
      incomplete chunked read). It was the last raw node still calling llm.invoke()
      without invoke_with_transient_retry — and it's the mode the `user` role gets.
[x] - Fix "Error resuming after question:" — a blank cause. httpx.ReadError (the
      provider dropping the socket mid-response) carries an EMPTY message, so five
      user-facing error frames rendered as a dangling colon, and the mothership row
      arrived with a blank Sample message. All of them now go through
      describe_exception(), which keeps the class name. Fingerprints unchanged.
      (Incident accf39ac3aa6; the crash itself was already fixed in 0.6.0d.)
[x] - Fix threads permanently 400ing with "insufficient tool messages" on DeepSeek. The orphaned-tool-call repair was creating the orphan it then couldn't see: dropping id-less tool calls via model_copy skips AIMessage's validators, so both parsed lists went empty and the serializer fell back to additional_kwargs["tool_calls"], announcing a call nothing answered. emitted_tool_calls() now reads that fallback (as its docstring always claimed), and id-less raw calls are pruned too.
[x] - Fix a Postgres restart killing the next agent turn on every box. Our checkpointer connection pools were built without psycopg's `check=`, so a connection whose backend had already died while idle in the pool was handed straight to the checkpointer and raised "consuming input failed: server closed the connection unexpectedly" mid-stream. Nothing retried it: the pool never re-checks a connection it has handed out, aput_writes is a bare executemany, and LangGraph's retry_policy covers node execution, not the checkpointer put — which runs in the Pregel loop teardown and aborts the whole astream. All three pools now check liveness at checkout and swap in a fresh connection.
[x] - Fix Database Mode (and every other mode) 400ing on Fireworks/GMI. Agents build
      their system prompt as Anthropic cache blocks (a LIST with cache_control), but
      those two OpenAI-compatible gateways require system content to be a plain
      string — and the fleet default deepseek-v4-flash is policy-remapped to
      Fireworks. New system_message_for_model() flattens the blocks for non-Anthropic
      and leaves Claude's caching intact; applied in DynamicModelMiddleware (sync +
      async, covers all create_agent modes) and at the call sites that run no
      middleware (ai_builder, beginner, plain_chat, both sub-agent factories). Sweep
      test fails any new agent that copy-pastes the cached-prompt idiom without it.
      Notes: docs/dev/provider_prompt_shape.md
[x] - Add a cookbook search if you do /cookbook -> then it brings up a list of all the cookbook entries from llamapress.ai/cookbook and if you type after /cookbook then it filters/searches through them - make sense? From right within the chat.html 
[.] - I want a way to see the .env headers (not the actual values), and to enable the ability to toggle certain .env variables with switches, such as the view model dropdown. I also want to be able to see a list of enabled models in the settings regardless of whether the setting is configured or not - make sense?
[] - I want a way to add my own CUSTOM .env variables that can get saved in a database and merged in so that they make it into the LlamaPress-Simple containers
[x] - Fix the rainbow UI. Engineer and Beginner mode both shipped pages where every
      category got its own saturated color, statuses were solid badges, and heroes
      arrived with gradients nobody asked for. Three lines in the prompts caused it:
      "Build something visually impressive" (read as "add more colors"), its example
      list `btn-primary` / `badge-success` / `alert-warning` (read as "use a different
      one per thing"), and Visual Polish Standards having sections for layout,
      typography, motion and icons but NONE for color — so nothing ever told the agent
      to hold back. All three modes now carry the same Color restraint rules: one brand
      color + neutral surfaces, red/amber/green reserved for destructive/warning/success
      only, at most one brand accent plus one semantic alert color per section (status
      columns and legends excepted — that's meaning, not decoration), subtle tinted
      status over solid badges, and NO gradients unless the project brand guide
      explicitly allows them (no brand guide = no gradients). "Visually impressive" is
      now "visually excellent", explicitly defined as spacing, hierarchy, typography,
      alignment, borders and subtle motion — not color. Edited:
      rails_agent/prompts.py (covers Engineer, Engineer plan mode, and BOTH delegated
      sub-agents — they all reuse RAILS_AGENT_PROMPT, and delegated work is where most
      of the rainbow was coming from), rails_beginner_agent/prompts.py, and
      rails_plan_mode_agent/prompts.py (Beginner plan mode). Tinted-status examples use
      `bg-success/10 text-success` / `badge-outline` rather than DaisyUI 5's
      `badge-soft` — the skeleton pins daisyui@4.12.10, where that class does not exist.
      NOT touched: html_agent/prompts.py (LlamaPress HTML mode), which still teaches
      "vibrant gradients" and gradient hero sections in its worked examples.
[x] - Teach the agents the new theme-based branding. Brand colors are now defined ONCE
      in `rails/app/assets/stylesheets/application.css` as two DaisyUI themes
      (`[data-theme="llamapress"]` light / `[data-theme="llamapress-dark"]` dark), and
      every page inherits them through semantic classes — but no prompt mentioned that
      file even once, so "make the buttons purple" got answered by restyling a page,
      and nothing forbade the one thing that actually breaks the inheritance: a hex
      code (or `bg-indigo-600`, or `bg-white`/`text-gray-900`, which look fine in light
      mode and break dark) written into a view. Engineer, Beginner and Plan mode now
      carry the same two rules — colors live in the theme, edit BOTH blocks; never
      hardcode a color in a view, semantic names only. Engineer plan mode and both
      delegated sub-agents inherit it via RAILS_AGENT_PROMPT.
      The rule is deliberately self-healing — "if the file has no `[data-theme=` blocks
      yet, add them" — because `rails/app` is client-owned and NOT in bin/update's
      ALLOWLIST, so the ~45 existing boxes will never receive the theme by sync. They
      migrate themselves the first time someone asks for a color change; new boxes ship
      with it. No skeleton change and no release are involved (LlamaPress-Simple is
      irrelevant here — docker-compose mounts ./rails/app over the image).
      Also fixed the thing that would have beaten the prompt rule: brand.json's palette
      was injected as bare hex codes ("Colors: Primary #5b21b6") on every styling turn,
      which reads as paint to apply. build_brand_context() now appends BRAND_THEME_NOTE
      on BOTH progressive-disclosure branches (short guide inlined, long guide
      summarized), framing the palette as theme values applied by editing the theme.
      Tests: app/tests/test_theme_prompt_rules.py — structural only (each prompt names
      the stylesheet, both themes, and the hardcode ban; both brand-context branches
      carry the note), never assertions about model output.
      NOT done: no theme-editing tool, no brand.json→CSS generation, and ai_builder /
      pyxl / html_agent are untouched — they render standalone HTML off a Tailwind CDN,
      not the app's theme.